   8: 
   9: # Presence Safety
  10: 
  11: Presence is a distributed, concurrency-sensitive subsystem. Database state and
  12: locked database functions are authoritative. Browser state, Realtime payloads,
  13: and local storage are hints or invalidation signals only.
  14: 
  15: ## Read before acting
  16: 
  17: Select references by the behavior or contract affected. Read the state model
  18: before changing runtime behavior; documentation edits need only the references
  19: whose instructions they affect.
  20: 
  21: | Task concerns | Read |
  22: | --- | --- |
  23: | Authority, identity, connection, occupancy, or finding owners | [State model and 
ownership](references/state-model.md) |
  24: | Movement, leases, logout, or account/company lifecycle | [Movement and session 
transitions](references/transitions.md) |
  25: | Private access, membership revisions, capacity, or Knock | [Access and 
capacity](references/access-capacity.md) |
  26: | Subscriptions, reconnects, caches, or runtime diagnosis | [Realtime and 
debugging](references/realtime-debugging.md) |
  27: | Selecting checks, reviewing changes, or editing these instructions | [Testing and 
evidence](references/testing.md) |
  28: | Live verification, incidents, cutover, or rollout | [Active limitations](references/known-issues.md) |
  29: 
  30: ## Non-negotiable invariants
  31: 
  32: 1. The database is the authority for leases, placement, access, and capacity.
  33: 2. Connection is derived from live leases. Space occupancy additionally needs
  34:    matching active placement and session/version state.
  35: 3. Availability status is not connection truth.
  36: 4. All movement goes through one client coordinator and one atomic database
  37:    transition. Never add a parallel writer or check-then-write path.
  38: 5. Automatic placement cannot override a newer manual action.
  39: 6. Private access, membership revision, capacity, placement, and Knock
  40:    consumption are decided under the same database locks.
  41: 7. Realtime is an invalidation mechanism. Reconcile from the authoritative
  42:    snapshot after subscribe, reconnect, drift, or ambiguous events.
  43: 8. Every cache, storage key, channel, mutation, and invalidation is scoped by
  44:    both company and application user identity.
  45: 9. A successful HTTP response means the committed transition result, not merely
  46:    that a request was accepted.
  47: 10. Retries have a capped delay, cancellation, and explicit classification.
  48:     Missing database contracts, permission failures, and invalid state surface as
  49:     actionable incompatibilities instead of generic retryable errors.
  50: 
  51: ## Presence database compatibility
  52: 
  53: For changes that depend on database behavior, establish the affected contract
  54: from source and migrations before implementation. Verify it on the named
  55: target before applying changes or claiming runtime compatibility:
  56: 
  57: - session, heartbeat, disconnect, transition, snapshot, reconciliation, and
  58:   Knock RPC names, signatures, grants, and result shapes;
  59: - runtime-control mode, required indexes, triggers, RLS, cron, lease timing, and
  60:   migration order;
  61: - a rollout order that never points new application code at a missing database
  62:   contract.
  63: 
  64: Do not classify a missing RPC, incompatible signature, disabled runtime mode, or
  65: permission contract as a generic transient failure. Surface the incompatibility,
  66: stop the affected workflow, and report the application as not ready until the
  67: named database and deployment are compatible.
  68: 
  69: Online changes, authorization, readback, rollback, rollout states, and reporting
  70: are governed by [AGENTS.md](../../../AGENTS.md).
  71: 
  72: ## Implementation and verification
  73: 
  74: Map the affected readers, writers, identities, and authority boundaries, using
  75: the implementation map in the state-model reference. Reproduce or characterize
  76: reported failures before editing. Preserve the invariants and reuse the
  77: existing coordinator and RPC boundary.
  78: 
  79: Select adversarial coverage for the behavior being changed: stale state,
  80: duplicate requests, account/company switches, retries, reconnects, multi-tab
  81: ordering, and real concurrency where affected. Use the testing reference for
  82: required checks and review gates; document-only edits use its instruction checks.
  83: 
  84: For runtime work, report which affected workflows are usable or still
  85: unverified. For database/rollout work, include the named target, required
  86: migration/RPC/runtime contract, readback evidence, and deployment compatibility.
  87: 
  88: ## Conditions that block dependent work
  89: 
  90: Stop the affected action and investigate when:
  91: 
  92: - a required database contract is unknown or incompatible on the target;
  93: - a second movement writer or check-then-write flow is being introduced;
  94: - a target-scoped cache, channel, or mutation lacks company and user identity;
  95: - a production or shared-test action is destructive or changes global mode;
  96: - tests prove mocks but not the concurrency or Realtime behavior being claimed;
  97: - code and database cannot be rolled out in a compatible order.
  98: 
  99: Continue source analysis, local preparation, and independent checks that do not
 100: rely on the missing prerequisite. Keep the dependent runtime check, operation,
 101: or readiness claim explicitly blocked until the prerequisite is established.
 102: If source and these instructions disagree, reconcile the affected contract
 103: before changing that behavior; do not invent a second authority.

