   1: # Delegation — OpenAI orchestrator
   2: 
   3: Read this file when the orchestrating model is from the OpenAI family (Codex CLI
   4: or another GPT-based agent) and it needs to hand work to a Claude model. If a
   5: Claude-family model is orchestrating, read `delegation-anthropic.md` instead.
   6: 
   7: The asymmetry that matters: Claude Code has a Codex plugin, but Codex has no
   8: equivalent Claude plugin. So this side drives Claude through the `claude` CLI
   9: inside a WezTerm pane. WezTerm's mux behaves like tmux and keeps the pane alive
  10: after the calling turn ends, which is what makes a long review survivable.
  11: 
  12: ## Model and effort policy
  13: 
  14: | Work delegated to Anthropic | Model ID | Effort |
  15: | --- | --- | --- |
  16: | Standard work, when delegation is explicitly requested | `claude-opus-5-5` | `high` |
  17: | Important work: adversarial review, review gates, acceptance checks | `claude-opus-5-5` | `xhigh` |
  18: 
  19: Use Claude Opus 5.5 explicitly. The moving `opus` alias is not a version pin.
  20: Pass `--model claude-opus-5-5 --effort high` for standard work and
  21: `--model claude-opus-5-5 --effort xhigh` for important work.
  22: 
  23: ## What gets delegated
  24: 
  25: - **Adversarial review: delegate to Claude Opus 5.5 at `xhigh`.** Every diff
  26:   that is about to be handed to the user gets this independent adversarial review.
  27: - **Implementation, debugging, and research stay local** unless the user
  28:   explicitly requests delegation. Important delegated work follows the criteria
  29:   in AGENTS.md and uses `xhigh`.
  30: - Never silently substitute another model or lower the required effort. If the
  31:   runtime rejects the model or effort, report that limitation and keep the
  32:   dependent review pending; do not call it accepted.
  33: 
  34: ## The review prompt
  35: 
  36: Give the reviewer the diff and the claims, not just the files. A useful
  37: adversarial prompt states:
  38: 
  39: 1. what the change is supposed to do, in one paragraph;
  40: 2. the exact diff range (`git diff <base>...HEAD`, or the branch name);
  41: 3. that spec conformance plus green tests is not acceptance — the reviewer must
  42:    attack the diff and independently verify every claim made in the code
  43:    comments, commit messages, and summary;
  44: 4. the repository traps that matter here: `users.id` versus `users.supabase_uid`,
  45:    server versus browser Supabase client, `getUser` versus `getSession`, RLS
  46:    coverage, service-role exposure, and Realtime or presence regressions;
  47: 5. the required output shape: findings ranked by severity, each with a concrete
  48:    failure scenario, plus an explicit verdict of approve or revision required.
  49: 
  50: ## WezTerm mechanics — verified on this machine
  51: 
  52: WezTerm lives at `C:\Program Files\WezTerm\wezterm.exe`. Quote the path; it
  53: contains a space.
  54: 
  55: - `wezterm cli` with **no mux or GUI already running will block** while it tries
  56:   to auto-start one. Always pass `--no-auto-start`, and expect a non-zero exit if
  57:   nothing is running rather than a hang.
  58: - `wezterm cli --no-auto-start list` prints the `WINID TABID PANEID` table. Use
  59:   it to find an existing pane id before spawning.
  60: - `wezterm cli spawn` needs a reference pane to resolve the domain and window.
  61:   From a non-interactive shell `$WEZTERM_PANE` is unset, so pass
  62:   `--pane-id <existing>` explicitly or the command fails with
  63:   `--pane-id was not specified`. On success it prints only the new pane id.
  64: - `wezterm cli --no-auto-start get-text --pane-id <id>` reads the pane's visible
  65:   scrollback. Panes default to 80x24, so **do not treat `get-text` as the source
  66:   of truth** — long review output gets clipped and wrapped. Use it only for
  67:   liveness checks.
  68: - `wezterm cli --no-auto-start kill-pane --pane-id <id>` cleans up. Kill the
  69:   panes you spawned; do not leave a stray mux server behind.
  70: 
  71: ## The delegation recipe
  72: 
  73: Redirect the reviewer's output to a file and read the file. That is the only
  74: reliable capture path.
  75: 
  76: ```bash
  77: WT="/c/Program Files/WezTerm/wezterm.exe"
  78: REPO="E:/projects/virtual-office"
  79: OUT="$REPO/docs/<topic-folder>/opus-review.md"
  80: 
  81: # 1. Find a reference pane (spawn a mux first if the table is empty).
  82: "$WT" cli --no-auto-start list
  83: 
  84: # 2. Spawn the review in its own pane; capture the printed pane id.
  85: PANE=$("$WT" cli --no-auto-start spawn --pane-id 0 --cwd "$REPO" -- \
  86:   bash -lc "claude -p \"\$(cat $REPO/review-prompt.txt)\" \
  87:     --model claude-opus-5-5 --effort xhigh --permission-mode plan > \"$OUT\" 2>&1")
  88: 
  89: # 3. Poll for completion by watching the output file, not the pane text.
  90: # 4. Read $OUT, then clean up:
  91: "$WT" cli --no-auto-start kill-pane --pane-id "$PANE"
  92: ```
  93: 
  94: Notes on the `claude` invocation:
  95: 
  96: - `-p` / `--print` runs one shot and exits — the right mode for a review.
  97: - `--model claude-opus-5-5 --effort xhigh` pins the required review model and effort.
  98: - Keep the reviewer read-only. `--permission-mode plan` or an `--allowedTools`
  99:   list restricted to read and search tools prevents it from editing the diff it
 100:   is supposed to be judging.
 101: - Put the prompt in a file rather than inlining it. Long prompts through nested
 102:   shell quoting on Windows are a reliable source of silent truncation.
 103: - A pane that exits immediately with no output usually means the CLI never
 104:   started. Check the pane text before assuming the review found nothing.
 105: 
 106: ## Reporting back
 107: 
 108: Delegation does not change the reporting contract in `AGENTS.md`. Pane ids,
 109: model names, and prompt files are internal machinery: keep them out of the
 110: user's report unless one is genuinely needed, and then confine it to the single
 111: `Technical note:` line. Report an unresolved review finding as a remaining risk;
 112: never present a diff as accepted when the reviewer asked for revision.

